          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;p&gt;Fill in the blanks following each description with the following types of conflict: &lt;strong&gt;approach-approach conflict, avoidance-avoidance conflict, &lt;/strong&gt;or&lt;strong&gt; approach-avoidance conflict.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;1. Conflict whereby one must choose between two more or less equally undesirable or unattractive goals.&lt;/p&gt; _______ 2. Choose between doing unwanted homework or doing unwanted house chores _______ 3. Whatever choice is made will not be satisfying, so you simply have to decide which is the "lesser of two evils", and hence the least stressful. _______ 4. Conflict whereby&amp;nbsp;one must choose between two desirable or attractive goals. _______ 5. Choose between two appealing destinations for your vacation, for example, Mexico vs. the Caribbean. _______ 6. one of the least stressful conflicts, although in situations where you need to make important decisions (e.g. deciding between two good career opportunities), it can result in some increased stress. It typically results from limitations on one's time, space, energy and personal and financial resources. _______ 7. A fairly common type of conflict that can provoke a significant level of stress. In these types of situations, people tend to vacilate, going back and forth between decisions. They are sometimes referred to as a "mixed blessing" because you have to accept some negative and some positive features no matter which choice is made _______ 8. Tanya has been with Company X for 12 years. She is offered a job promotion as vice president of the company. It would pay her much more than she is currently making. However, if she was to accept this position, she would have to move to a city that was far away from her extended family and where the weather was much colder. _______ 9. Conflict involving a decision regarding whether to pursue or avoid something that has both positive and negative aspects to it. _______ </mattext>
